    "lawCode": "Add §1506-d, amd §1507, N-PC L; amd §291, Town L",
    "lawSection": "Not-for-Profit Corporation Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S1876A\n\nSPONSOR: COMRIE\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the not-for-profit corporation law and\nthe town law, in relation to the abandonment of cemeteries\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nThe bill would create a process through which the state and its locali-\nties are notified of the pending abandonment of state regulated ceme-\nteries. This notice allows time to work towards a reorganization of the\ncemetery corporation to prevent abandonment, and helps to provide an\norderly transition, if necessary, to municipal control to limit fiscal\nimpacts on local governments.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 adds a new section 1506-d to the Not-for-Profit Corporation\nLaw outlining cemetery abandonment notification requirements and assist-\nance available to prevent abandonment. Any state-regulated cemetery that\nis unable to maintain operations and faces abandonment will be required\nto provide notice to the State Cemetery Board and the local municipality\nin which the cemetery is located, at least 180 days in advance of a\npotential abandonment. This notification will trigger State Cemetery\nBoard involvement in an effort to prevent the abandonment.\n\nIn the event that abandonment is not preventable or operations cannot be\ntransferred to another regulated and solvent cemetery and the condition\nof the cemetery operation prevents immediate local takeover of the ceme-\ntery, a request for the appointment of a temporary receiver may be made\nto the Attorney General with the consent of the local government to\nmanage the cemetery operations until a transfer is possible. Any costs\nfor such receiverships would come from the already established state\nabandoned cemetery fund.\n\nSection 2 amends section 1507(h)(4) of the Not-for-Profit Corporation\nLaw to allow municipalities and other solvent not-for-profit cemetery\ncorporations that assumed control of abandoned cemeteries after January\n1st, 2015 to file an application to the abandoned cemetery fund 25 years\nafter assuming maintenance of abandoned cemeteries for updating repairs\non previously constructed cemetery structures that may need additional\nrepair.\n\nSection 3 would amend section 291 of the Town Law by adding a new subdi-\nvision (5) to limit damages for prior actions that occurred before muni-\ncipalities take over abandoned cemeteries.\n\nSection 4 states that this bill shall take effect immediately.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nAfter many years of fiscal stress and the lack of new volunteers, many\nfinancially fragile state-regulated cemeteries face abandonment. Under\nstate law, these abandoned cemeteries become the responsibility of local\nmunicipalities.\n\nMost municipal governments are unprepared or unable to take over these\ncemeteries immediately. In many instances, with little or no warning,\nlocal governments find themselves responsible for cemetery operations.\nThis bill provides for a 180 day process that allows time for efforts to\navoid cemetery abandonment. If efforts to prevent abandonment prove\nunsuccessful, it also provides for an orderly transfer of cemetery oper-\nations and access by local governments to the state's abandoned cemetery\nfund to pay for costs associated with this transition.\n\nThe maintenance and operation of abandoned cemeteries also requires\nlong-term upkeep and repairs that may not have been discovered at the\ntime of the abandonment. This legislation allows municipalities or other\nqualifying solvent cemeteries that have assumed responsibility for aban-\ndoned cemeteries to apply for additional funds, 25 years after their\ninitial application, to assist with repair and maintenance of cemetery\nstructures existing at the time of the abandonment.\n\nAdditionally, since municipalities take over maintenance of abandoned\ncemeteries and do not assume title to the property, this bill provides\nfor protection of the municipality for assuming this control.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nA.10515 of 2018, veto memo 295.\n\nSame as S.7835-A of 2018, veto memo 295.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act will take effect immediately.\n",
