    "memoText": "\n \n                         VETO MESSAGE - No. 128\n \nTO THE SENATE:\n \nI am returning herewith, without my approval, the following bill:\n \nSenate Bill Number 1876-A, entitled:\n \n    \"AN  ACT  to  amend  the not-for-profit corporation law and the town\n      law, in relation to the abandonment of cemeteries\"\n \n    NOT APPROVED\n \n  This bill would amend the Not-For-Profit Corporation Law and the  Town\nLaw  to  establish a notification process for cemetery corporations that\nare anticipating abandonment, provide for additional remedial assistance\nfrom the Department of State's Cemetery  Board,  and  limit  damages  to\nentities that assume responsibility for abandoned cemeteries.\n \n  Current  law  allows a municipality or solvent not-for-profit cemetery\ncorporation that assumes responsibility for  an  abandoned  cemetery  to\nseek  grant funding for necessary repairs. The source of the grant fund-\ning is a trust fund dedicated to  vandalism,  abandonment  and  monument\nrepair or removal (\"the Abandonment Fund\"). This legislation would allow\nany  such entity, which assumed responsibility for an abandoned cemetery\nafter January first, two thousand fifteen, to apply for a  second  grant\n25  years  after initial funding in order to repair existing structures.\nThere are currently almost 50 cemeteries that could  \"double  dip\"  into\nthe Abandonment Fund by using it as a source to indefinitely fund opera-\ntion and maintenance activities.\n \n  This  legislation  would  also  require--in  the absence of conditions\nprecedent--that the Attorney General  seek  judicial  appointment  of  a\nreceiver  for an abandoned cemetery until the cemetery is in a condition\nto be maintained by the municipality. The legislation assigns  unquanti-\nfied costs of such receivership to the Abandonment Fund.\n \n  As  I noted last year in my veto message, this legislation would place\nthe burden to fund indefinite receivership and additional repairs  on  a\nfund that is already overcommitted without providing either an appropri-\nation  or  a fee to cover the anticipated increase in spending.  While I\nappreciate that the sponsors attempted to resolve the concerns I identi-\nfied last year, those attempts do  not  go  far  enough  to  obviate  an\nunfunded State fiscal. For these reasons, I am again constrained to veto\nthe  bill.  However,  I  direct  the Department of State to evaluate the\nefficacy of conducting  an  administrative  rulemaking  to  address  the\nobjectives  identified  in  the  bill that can be adopted without legis-\nlation.\n \n  The bill is disapproved.                    (signed) ANDREW M. CUOMO\n \n                               __________\n",
    "signedDate": null,
